.TH DMESG 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
dmesg \- print the kernel's log
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dmesg
.RB [ \-w ]
.RB [ \-c " | " \-C ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dmesg
prints the kernel's log, oldest first: devices joining and leaving the bus,
each event the kernel takes from a device (a motion, a key, a frame), mounts,
a process that crashed (past its own error handler: a program's error is
reported by the program, as an exit), and the kernel's own messages. It keeps the
last 200.
.PP
Each line is an entry, as OpenOS's
.B dmesg
showed events: the time by the world's clock, in brackets; where it came
from (a device's name as
.BR lsdev (1)
gives it, or
.BR mount ,
.BR kernel ,
a process as its path and pid); the device's id; and what happened. On a
terminal they're in color, OpenOS's: the time red, where green, the id
yellow.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-follow
Waits for more, printing each entry as it comes, until ^C.
.TP
.B \-c
Clears the log after printing it.
.TP
.B \-C
Clears the log, printing nothing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0; 1 for an option it does not know.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
dmesg
dmesg \-w
dmesg | grep storage
.fi
.SH NOTES
Linux's
.B dmesg
shows seconds since boot and the kernel's subsystems' messages; mcos's
shows the world's time, and the devices' events as OpenOS's did.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lsdev (1)
